{"lecture_id":"2026-06-29-M1-3","lecture_summary":"Circular-motion dynamics: relate angular and linear motion, distinguish radial from tangential acceleration, identify the real forces producing inward acceleration, and apply radial free-body equations to Ferris-wheel and friction-limited turntable problems.","videos":[{"rank":1,"title":"Rotational Motion Physics, Basic Introduction, Angular Velocity & Tangential Acceleration","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WQ9AH2S8B6Y","reason":"Establishes the angular-to-linear relationships, period and frequency, and the distinction among tangential and centripetal acceleration needed for both lecture examples."},{"rank":2,"title":"Normal Force on a Hill, Centripetal Force, Roller Coaster Problem, Vertical Circular Motion, Physics","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ec6btKP4qfg","reason":"The closest match to the Ferris-wheel analysis: draw radial free-body diagrams and determine how normal force changes between the top and bottom of a vertical circle."},{"rank":3,"title":"Static Friction, Centripetal Force, Circular Motion, Car Rounding Curve & Rotor Ride Physics Problem","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=dMgyy95uJUg","reason":"Covers static friction as the real inward force and the limiting-friction condition for maximum circular speed; its flat-curve calculation is mathematically equivalent to the turntable coin problem."}],"coverage_gaps":["No playlist entry explicitly derives the coin-on-a-turntable result in angular form, ω_max = √(μ_s g/r); video 3 requires converting its equivalent linear-speed result using v = ωr.","The Ferris-wheel seat geometry is not named directly; video 2 uses equivalent roller-coaster and hill normal-force problems.","The lecture’s brief constant-angular-acceleration equation review is not explicit in this minimal set. The playlist has a focused rotational-kinematics video, but adding it would mainly cover ancillary review material.","Symbolic-first solving, unit checks, and consistently choosing inward as the positive radial direction are lecture-specific emphases not clearly guaranteed by the playlist descriptions."]}
